 ],
 "literal": "Like the kala fruit, black-stemmed small lute's / sweet song bards because they presented, / male elephants are not there which became rough-trunked tall tethering posts, / forest peacocks with their flocks to roost, / with the women wearing jewels rare to give, / has declined, they say, Āy's palace, / to the taste sweet seasoned food / to others giving without, their own stomach feeding, / praise-worthy lofty fame abandoned, / drum possessing wealthy people's mansion is not like indeed!",
 "translation": "Because bards have taken away all his male elephants, singing sweet songs to the accompaniment of the small, black-stemmed lute shaped like a kala fruit, the tall, rough-barked tethering posts in the palace of Āy, now empty of elephants, have become roosting places for forest peacocks and their flocks. Along with his women wearing jewels that are now rare to give away, Āy's palace has declined in wealth, they say. Yet, it is not at all like the mansions of those wealthy men who possess drums but have abandoned praise-worthy lofty fame, feeding only their own stomachs with delicious, seasoned food without giving any to others!",
 "modern": "களாப் பழத்தைப் போன்ற கரிய தண்டினைக் கொண்ட சிறிய யாழினை மீட்டி, இனிய பாடல்களைப் பாடிய பாணர்களுக்குப் பரிசிலாக வழங்கிவிட்டதால், ஆய் மன்னனின் அரண்மனையில் யானைகள் இல்லாமல் போயின. அதனால், கரடுமுரடான பட்டையையுடைய நெடிய யானை கட்டும் கம்பங்கள் வெறிச்சோடி, காட்டு மயில்கள் தம் கூட்டத்தோடு தங்குமிடங்களாக மாறிவிட்டன. வாரி வழங்குவதற்கு அரிய அணிகலன்களை அணிந்த தன் மனைவியரோடு ஆயின் அரண்மனை வறுமையுற்றுப் பொலிவிழந்தது என்று கூறுவர். ஆயினும், அது பிறருக்குக் கொடாமல் சுவையான தாளித்த உணவைத் தாங்களே உண்டு, புகழைத் துறந்து வாழும் முரசு கொண்ட செல்வர்களின் மாளிகைகளைப் போன்றது அன்று.",
 "explanation": "The poet Uṟaiyūr Eṇiccēri Muṭamōciyār praises the extraordinary generosity of the chieftain Vēḷ Āy Aṇṭiraṉ. He describes how Āy has given away all his male elephants to the bards who sang sweet songs with their small, black-stemmed lutes. As a result, the tall tethering posts in his palace yard stand empty and have become roosting places for wild forest peacocks. Even though Āy's palace has now declined in material wealth, the poet asserts that it is far superior to the mansions of selfish rich men. These wealthy men possess grand war drums but live without fame, stuffing themselves with delicious seasoned food without sharing it with anyone.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
 "generated": "2026-11-26"
}